      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** The matrix groups, standard Hermitian inner product on $\\mathbb C^N$, and last-column map in the Statement. Work in ZF + AC for the imported numerable-bundle fibration result.\n\n[F1] A locally trivial fiber bundle over a base with a supplied numeration is a Hurewicz fibration under the stated Axiom of Choice hypothesis. [[def-locally-trivial-fiber-bundle]], [[thm-numerable-fiber-bundles-are-hurewicz-fibrations]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]\n\n[F2] A smooth partition of unity subordinate to the finite trivializing cover supplies a numeration of that cover. [[def-smooth-partition-of-unity-subordinate-to-an-open-cover]]\n\n\n\n1.1 Fix $v_0\\in S^{2N-1}$. Choose an orthonormal basis $w_1,\\ldots,w_{N-1}$ of $v_0^\\perp$ so the matrix $(w_1,\\ldots,w_{N-1},v_0)$ has determinant $1$. For $v$ in a sufficiently small neighborhood $U$ of $v_0$, project each $w_i$ onto $v^\\perp$ and apply Gram–Schmidt in that fixed order. The projected vectors remain linearly independent after shrinking $U$, because their Gram determinant is continuous and equals $1$ at $v_0$. The resulting orthonormal frame depends smoothly on $v$. Multiply its first vector by the inverse of the determinant of the frame if necessary; this scalar has modulus one and the adjustment is smooth, preserves orthonormality, and makes the determinant exactly one. Thus $s(v)\\in SU(N)$ is a smooth local section of $p$ on $U$. [given, algebra, construct]\n\n2.1 For $g\\in p^{-1}(U)$ with last column $v$, the matrix $s(v)^{-1}g$ fixes the last basis vector. Unitarity makes its orthogonal complement invariant, so it is block diagonal $\\operatorname{diag}(A,1)$; determinant one gives $A\\in SU(N-1)$. The maps $$(A,v)\\longmapsto s(v)\\operatorname{diag}(A,1),\\qquad g\\longmapsto(\\operatorname{upperBlock}(s(p(g))^{-1}g),p(g))$$ are smooth inverses between $SU(N-1)\\times U$ and $p^{-1}(U)$. This proves local triviality with the asserted fiber. [step 1.1, construct]\n\n3.1 The sphere is compact, so finitely many such neighborhoods cover it; [F2] gives a smooth partition of unity and hence a numeration. Applying [F1] proves the bundle is a Hurewicz fibration. For $N=2$, the stabilizer of the last column in $SU(2)$ has its upper block in $SU(1)=\\{1\\}$, so the fiber is a point. [F1, F2, step 2.1, given] ∎"
    },
    {
      "file": "items/lem-qftx-low-unitary-homotopy-inputs.md",
      "item_id": "lem-qftx-low-unitary-homotopy-inputs",
      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** The column fibrations of [[lem-qftx-special-unitary-column-fibration]], the Hopf and suspension results below, and the imported sphere and fibration theorems. Work in ZF + AC because the numerable-bundle supplier uses AC.\n\n[F1] The column maps form Hurewicz fibrations $SU(N-1)\\to SU(N)\\to S^{2N-1}$. [[lem-qftx-special-unitary-column-fibration]]\n\n[F2] For a based Serre fibration, the homotopy groups and component sets of fiber, total space, and base form an exact sequence. [[thm-long-exact-sequence-of-homotopy-groups-of-a-fibration]]\n\n[F3] For $k<d$, every based map $S^k\\to S^d$ is nullhomotopic; degree identifies $\\pi_3(S^3)$ with $\\mathbb Z$. [[thm-lower-dimensional-sphere-maps-are-based-nullhomotopic]], [[thm-based-sphere-maps-are-classified-by-geometric-degree]]\n\n[F4] The suspension homomorphism $\\pi_3(S^2)\\to\\pi_4(S^3)$ is surjective by the Freudenthal boundary case. [[thm-freudenthal-suspension-theorem]]\n\n[F5] The Hopf generator $h$ generates $\\pi_3(S^2)$, $2[\\Sigma h]=0$, and $(\\Sigma h)\\circ(\\Sigma^2h)\\ne0$. [[lem-qftx-hopf-generator-and-cone]], [[lem-qftx-folded-product-attachment-suspends-to-zero]], [[lem-qftx-suspended-hopf-composite-adem-obstruction]]\n\n\n\n1.1 The unit-quaternion parametrization $q=a+bi+cj+dk\\mapsto\\begin{pmatrix}a+bi&c+di\\\\-c+di&a-bi\\end{pmatrix}$ is a continuous bijection from compact $S^3$ to Hausdorff $SU(2)$; direct multiplication shows it is unitary with determinant one, and every matrix in $SU(2)$ has this form. It is therefore a homeomorphism. By [F3], $\\pi_1(S^3)=\\pi_2(S^3)=0$ and $\\pi_3(S^3)=\\mathbb Z$. [given, F3, algebra]\n\n2.1 By [F5], the generator of $\\pi_3(S^2)$ suspends to an element $\\eta_3$ generating the quotient $\\pi_4(S^3)$ by [F4]. The second part of [F5] gives $2\\eta_3=0$. The nonzero composite $\\eta_3\\circ\\eta_4$ in [F5] implies $\\eta_3\\ne0$, since a composite with a null factor is null. Thus the cyclic quotient generated by $\\eta_3$ has exactly two elements, proving $\\pi_4(SU(2))\\cong\\mathbb Z/2$; the same nonzero composite lies in $\\pi_5(S^3)$, so that group is nonzero. [F4, F5, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Induct on $N\\ge3$. The base sphere $S^{2N-1}$ has dimension at least five, so [F3] makes its homotopy groups in degrees $1,2,3,4$ zero. Exactness [F2] at degrees one and two therefore gives $\\pi_1(SU(N-1))\\cong\\pi_1(SU(N))$ and $\\pi_2(SU(N-1))\\cong\\pi_2(SU(N))$; at degree three it gives $\\pi_3(SU(N-1))\\cong\\pi_3(SU(N))$. The component exact sequence and the connected fiber and base show $SU(N)$ is path connected: the map on components is onto, and its kernel is the image of the one-point component set of the fiber. Starting from [step 1.1], induction gives all three displayed low groups and connectedness for every $N$. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra] ∎"
